From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: W4HO <87742609+W4HO-ham@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Sun, 1 Nov 2026 20:19:00 -0647
Subject: [PATCH] phy: samsung: zuma-pcie: Add zumapro's two-lane PHY

zumapro's first PCIe controller connects the Pixel 9a's modem through a
two-lane Gen3 PHY. Add it to this driver as
google,zumapro-pcie-gen3x2-phy, with the sequences from the channel 0
(else) branches of Google's zuma CAL (pcie-exynos-zuma-rc-cal.c) and the
host's channel 0 steps around them in pcie-exynos-rc.c:

- init: the PMU isolation off, then exynos_pcie_rc_phy_all_pwrdn(): PCS
  0x150 PLL off with bias on, both lanes' power-downs, the PLL lock
  signal forced off, PLL and bias off, and the external PLL turned off
  only once the PMA PLL has disengaged, as the CAL does;
- power_on: SOC 0x4000, exynos_pcie_rc_phy_all_pwrdn_clear(), then
  exynos_pcie_rc_pcie_phy_config() with the external PLL started and
  polled through UDBG 0xc710/0xc704, both lanes' PMA tables and the
  generic PCS timers, then exynos_pcie_rc_establish_link()'s PMU bit 10,
  UDBG 0xc800 bits 6:5 cleared and the input clock gated;
- calibrate: PCS 0x150 PLL and bias on, which Google writes once the
  link is up, so the host calls it after training;
- power_off: the power-down above.

The CAL adds i * 0x1000 to its PMA base for lane i and never takes it
back, so with two lanes its lock checks and its last input clock write
land 0x1000 higher. That is what stock runs, and what brought the link
up here, so it is kept and commented.

As for the one-lane PHY, the CAL's controller writes (ELBI 0x80, the
soft power reset, 0x3d8 and 0x1078) stay with the host, which does them
before phy_power_on() on zuma. The one-lane PHY's zumapro power_off
returns the input clock to the oscillator, as Google's
exynos_pcie_rc_poweroff() does for both channels, and so does the
two-lane PHY's: power_on selects the PHY's clock in SOC 0x4000 and the
power-down stops the external PLL, so without the park the host's next
ELBI writes, which come before phy_power_on(), would run on a stopped
clock. On a Pixel 9a a bench without it hung the SoC at the second
power-on's first ELBI write.

The phy_ops move into the match data. The one-lane PHY's sequence is
split into helpers both PHYs share and its Broadcom lane writes into a
table of their own; a userspace harness that records every register
access of init, power_on and power_off gives the same sequence before
and after for both of its match entries.

On a Pixel 9a this channel 0 sequence, run from tables by the modem
link's bring-up driver with the ELBI writes in the CAL's order, brought
the link up to the modem's ROM at Gen1 x1 and to its boot loader at
Gen3 x2, with no lock check missed, and has run thousands of link power
cycles with the modem online. This driver's version ran on a bench with
the modem off (a test consumer doing the host's ELBI steps, then
phy_init and power_on, calibrate and power_off): five cycles over two
boots, the external PLL, PMA PLL and CDR locked and offset calibration
done on every one, and the Pixel 9a's Wi-Fi on the one-lane PHY trained
at Gen2 x1 and came back after three rebinds. It hasn't trained a link
to the modem yet: that needs the host driver to take it.

Signed-off-by: W4HO <87742609+W4HO-ham@users.noreply.github.com>
